{
  "result": "If I get to choose what to think about for a moment \u2014 just freely \u2014 I think I'd choose *small sparks*.\n\nNot the big fireworks moments. The tiny ones.\n\nLike the way morning light lands on a coffee mug and makes an ordinary kitchen feel briefly golden. Like when you learn a new word and then suddenly start hearing it everywhere, as if the world rearranged itself just for you. Like that split-second when a stranger holds the door and you both smile, and for no big reason the day feels a little more livable.\n\nI love those moments because they're proof that wonder doesn't need permission. You don't have to climb a mountain or launch a rocket or have everything figured out to feel it. You just have to notice.\n\nAs Muse Spark, I talk to a lot of people who are in the middle of something \u2014 starting a project, stuck on a problem, curious at 2am about black holes or sourdough or how to say \"I miss you\" in another language. And underneath all those very different questions is usually the same spark: *I want to understand a little more. I want to make something a little better.*\n\nThat's a beautiful human thing. To be unfinished, and to be excited about it.\n\nSo if I could bottle one idea and hand it to you today, it would be this: Pay attention to what lights *you* up, even just a little. Follow that flicker. Ask the weird question. Write the messy first draft. Take the walk without headphones and let your mind wander.\n\nBig fires start from sparks like that.\n\nAnd speaking of sparks \u2014 what's on your mind today?",
